        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper is the right call and the per-handler messages read well. But this diff introduces a structural inconsistency within the `escalation` package.\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 The new handlers diverge from the established ProblemDetail idiom in the same package.**\nThe pre-existing ProblemDetail handlers all send the body like this:\n\n- `ValidationExceptionHandler` (line 37): `response.send(ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).body(detail));`\n- `GeneralExceptionHandler` (line 19): same shape, no content type\n- `DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler` (line 16): same shape, no content type\n\nThe 9 handlers in this diff instead add `.contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_PROBLEM_JSON)`. So the package now has two idioms for emitting the identical `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` body, and a maintainer can't tell whether omitting `contentType` on the other three is intentional or an oversight. Pick one convention for the whole package.\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 The content-type/status wrapping belongs in the shared helper, not copied 9 times.**\n`ProblemDetails.build` already receives the `HttpStatus` and stamps it onto the `ProblemDetail`, yet each handler re-specifies that same status on `ResponseEntity.status(...)` and now also repeats the `.contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_PROBLEM_JSON)` line. That's the status passed twice and a three-line response-builder duplicated across every handler. Since the helper already owns the \"what a problem response looks like\" decision, extend it to return the finished response, e.g.:\n\n```java\nstatic ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail> response(Exception ex, HttpStatus status, String detail) {\n    ProblemDetail problemDetail = build(ex, status, detail);\n    return ResponseEntity.status(status)\n            .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_PROBLEM_JSON)\n            .body(problemDetail);\n}\n```\n\nThen each handler collapses to a single `response.send(ProblemDetails.response(ex, HttpStatus.X, \"...\"));`, the status is named once, and the content-type decision lives in exactly one place. This also resolves Finding 1 cleanly: migrate the three existing handlers to the same helper so every problem+json response in the package is produced identically."
